50 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536799 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee33dd50-ad62-5049-ba5a-f3ae30d9952b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536807 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536807 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68800" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536807 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536807 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt{26}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536807 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05e49986-b987-5b1e-8762-a91ca97898e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536807 #edb/ref 1074 "The square root of a product of two positive numbers is equal to the product of the square roots of the numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{26}=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 13}=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{13}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536819 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536819 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536819 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536819 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $(-\\frac{12}{5})÷(-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536819 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b21718f-1527-5624-995c-489290485c6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536819 #edb/ref 1074 "Dividing a negative number by a negative number gives a positive number. Hence, the result will be positive.\n\nTherefore, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{5}÷3=\\frac{12}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{12}{15}=\\frac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{12}{5})÷(-3)=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536824 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536824 #edb/ref 1069 "q-318354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536824 #edb/ref 1071 "What number is $175%$ greater than $48?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32edbf40-91ab-5dc2-911c-42534beb5cff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536824 #edb/ref 1074 "To find a number $175%$ *greater than* $48,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+175%=275%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n275%=\\frac{275}{100}=2.75\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $175%$ greater than $48,$ we multiply $48$ by $2.75:$\n\n$$\n2.75\\cdot 48=132\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $175%$ greater than $48$ is $132.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536825 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536825 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536825 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f2/f2d269fab1c7b0f7206ead91739a6df41c931089edb355bded226c868c1b74fc.png)\n\nWhich sides are consecutive to $\\overset{―}{FH}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3496c0a1-50f2-52f3-ab73-961afb8b5fec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536825 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sides are consecutive if they have a vertex in common. Therefore, $\\overset{―}{AF}$ and $\\overset{―}{EH}$ are consecutive sides to $\\overset{―}{FH}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536830 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536830 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536830 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536830 #edb/ref 1071 "${(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536830 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4305d4d8-cadf-560e-80db-63776f30d2a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536830 #edb/ref 1074 "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${5}^{3}=125,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{125}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536851 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536851 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536851 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536851 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536851 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/87/878fd1c82e5d634e9090d9e7b49faf3a92135c8679d309e72505af6d201d5e80.png)\n\nThe inequality corresponding to the highlighted region shown above is $x${{field-1}}\n$-2\\text{ or }x$\n{{field-2}}$1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536851 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a9682b8-c5c3-5a53-a91f-bba592503325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536851 #edb/ref 1074 "The red rays contain all values less than $-2$ or greater than $1.$ In addition, \n\n- the open circle at $-2$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $-2,$ and\n\n- the open circle at $1$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $2.$\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents $x<-2$ or $x>1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536853 #edb/ref 1071 "$3{w}^{4}-{w}^{3}-5{w}^{4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b3fdd80-83cc-5d53-85c1-0216b4942139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536853 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, $3{w}^{3}$ and $-5{w}^{3}$are like terms, so we group them, and then we add or subtract the coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{w}^{4}-{w}^{3}-5{w}^{4} & = \\\\ (3{w}^{4}-5{w}^{4})-{w}^{3} & = \\\\ (3-5){w}^{4}-{w}^{3} & = \\\\ -2{w}^{4}-{w}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536855 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536855 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68992" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536855 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536855 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{6}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536855 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8bf7e480-fb51-579e-b130-139151e7ecc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536855 #edb/ref 1074 "The square root of a quotient of two positive numbers is equal to the quotient of the square roots of the numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{6}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536857 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536857 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536857 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{2}{5}x+4y+\\frac{5}{2}x+3y=${{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536857 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e8a0eda-6609-511f-9732-1a6312007048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536857 #edb/ref 1074 "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}x+4y+\\frac{5}{2}x+3y & = \\\\ (\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{5}{2}x)+(4y+3y) & = \\\\ (\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2})x+(4+3)y & = \\\\ (\\frac{4}{10}+\\frac{25}{10})x+7y & = \\\\ (\\frac{4+25}{10})x+7y & = \\\\ \\frac{29}{10}\\,x+7\\,y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536858 #edb/ref 1071 "The mass $m$ of a tablet PC measures $314.7\\,\\text{g}$ to the nearest tenth of a gram. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f0c5a6d-560b-5b20-a886-377893e98cd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536858 #edb/ref 1074 "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{g}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{g}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{g}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =314.7\\,\\text{g}-0.05\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =314.65\\,\\text{g} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =314.7\\,\\text{g}+0.05\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =314.75\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $m$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n314.65\\,\\text{g}\\le m<314.75\\,\\text{g}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-179536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536859 #edb/ref 1071 "If $r=4,$ then $\\frac{2r}{3}-2=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fef57fc-2f55-531e-87c9-45485e34049e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536859 #edb/ref 1074 "Replacing $r$ with $4$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2r}{3}-2 & = \\\\ \\frac{2(4)}{3}-2 & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}-2 & = \\\\ \\frac{8-3\\cdot 2}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536867 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536867 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536867 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536867 #edb/ref 1071 "${(-6\\times 9)}^{8}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536867 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a728363a-21de-5465-8925-c66dae64d6b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536867 #edb/ref 1074 "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $8$, we can raise each number to the power of $8,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n{(-6\\times 9)}^{8}={((-6)\\times 9)}^{8}={(-6)}^{8}\\times {9}^{8}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536871 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536871 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536871 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536871 #edb/ref 1071 "Maria has a bank account at LJB Bank. She has an overdraft facility authorized by this bank. Her bank balance on Monday was $-$40.00.$ To reduce her debt, Maria decides to make a deposit of $$18.60$ into her bank account on the following Tuesday. What is Maria's balance at the end of the day on Tuesday?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536871 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac3e396f-e9ff-515e-95c3-ddf87162b016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536871 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the balance at the end of the day on Tuesday, we must add the amount of money Maria deposited to the starting balance. This gives\n\n$$\n-$40.00+$18.60=-$21.40.\n$$\n\nThus, Maria's balance at the end of the day on Tuesday is $-$21.40.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536886 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536886 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536886 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536886 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0f/0f18036ac5790bda530116c6847243108f9617f9ae12fcbc5c1b2c3fff018985.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Parallelogram\n\nII. Rectangle\n\nIII. Rhombus" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536886 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d513115b-ad74-5e72-8ee2-71bd9f120a6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536886 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with two pairs of opposite congruent sides, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- Since the four angles of the quadrilateral are right angles, it is a rectangle.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral where the sides are not all congruent. So it is **not** a rhombus.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (parallelogram), and II (rectangle)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536897 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536897 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536897 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536897 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\frac{5x-10y-2z}{5}$ is written in the form $ax+by+cz,$ what are the values of  $a,$ $b$ and $c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536897 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3e4db00-9ed0-5e91-8213-2ca14aefffbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536897 #edb/ref 1074 "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-10y-2z}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5}-\\frac{10y}{5}-\\frac{2z}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{5}\\cdot x-\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot y-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot z & = \\\\ 1\\cdot x-2\\cdot y-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot z & = \\\\ x-2y-\\frac{2z}{5} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ax+by+cz,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(1)}_{⏟}}_{a}x+{{(-2)}_{⏟}}_{b}y+{{(-\\frac{2}{5})}_{⏟}}_{c}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $a=1,$ $b=-2$ and $c=-\\frac{2}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f37d062-2bc9-5789-a927-01f7e374eae9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186536818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536910 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536910 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e2425be-8098-53da-bfb3-6126a03f6f56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536910 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186536857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536911 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536911 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "207f17de-2281-5c47-9498-e8937f229514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536911 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186536807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536912 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186536979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536912 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "